# The null space of the whole descent under a fusion, taken twice. One row per fusion that has a
# resolved part: how deep its closure goes, how many fusions and how many leaf layers it reaches,
# the dimension of the composite map's null space, and the sum of the blocks below it.
#
# What the composite map is
#   One level of the fold sends a fusion's parts to its figure. Nesting composes those levels, so
#   the map from the LEAF layers under a root to that root's single figure is the product of the
#   levels between them, and `composition/descent.sqlc` is the relation that walks it. For maps
#   that are onto, the null space of a composite is the sum of the null spaces of its factors, so
#   the closure under a root owes two routes to one number and this relation reports both.
#   Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `kernel_closes`.
#
# The composite's own dimension is `leaves - 1`
#   The root is one figure, every level below it is onto, and a composite of onto maps is onto, so
#   the map from the leaves to that figure is onto a single dimension and its null space is
#   everything else. `rank/composition_image.sqlc` is where the surjectivity at one level is held;
#   without it this column is not the dimension of anything.
#
# The other route is the blocks, and they are summed over DISTINCT fusions
#   Each fusion below the root contributes its own block once, however many paths arrive at it, so
#   the walk is reduced to one row per reached layer before anything is summed. That reduction is
#   deliberate and it is what gives the two routes something to disagree about: a layer reached
#   twice raises the block sum through the fusion that reached it a second time while leaving the
#   leaf count exactly where it was.
#
# So the two agree exactly where the descent is a tree
#   A root whose closure has `L` leaves and `F` fusions and whose descent is a tree has `L + F - 1`
#   parts in it, and the blocks sum to `parts - F`, which is `L - 1`. Take either the second path
#   to a leaf or a cycle away from that and the arithmetic parts company. `checks/jagged_layer.sqlc`
#   accuses the first and `checks/layers_move_together.sqlc` reports the second, so this relation
#   is a third instrument over both, reading the closure rather than the siblings.
#
# The row space of the closure is the path product, and it is positive or it is unstated
#   The composite is onto one figure, so its row space is one dimension whatever the depth, and
#   the generator of it is the vector of PATH PRODUCTS, one per leaf, which
#   `composition/descent.sqlc` accumulates as it walks. Every factor on a path is strictly
#   positive, so every entry of that generator is too, and the closure inherits the block-level
#   property whole: no non-negative assignment to the leaves is invisible to the root.
#   `leaves_positive` and `leaves_unknown` are that statement counted, and they must exhaust the
#   leaves. A leaf reached through a part whose factor is a typed absence is in the second column,
#   because nobody has said what its path multiplies by, and that is a different fact from a
#   direction of zero.
#
# A fusion nothing resolved is a leaf here, and it is the right answer
#   `rank/composition_kernel.sqlc` has no row for a fusion whose every part names a filing this
#   corpus does not hold, so nothing descends through it and it is counted among the leaves. That
#   is what the matrix sees: no columns under it, nothing below it to reach.
#   `rank/composition_image.sqlc` is where the state itself is reported.
#
# Every fusion is a root of its own closure
#   Not only the fusions nothing composes. A fusion in the middle of a stack has a closure under
#   it and owes the same identity, so keying this on the tops of the trees would leave the
#   interior untested and would report one defect once per ancestor above it.
#
# Probe
#   `algebra/composition_closure.sqlc` holds the two routes together. In a rolled-back
#   transaction, give a fusion a part naming a layer already reachable beneath it through another
#   composition: the block sum rises, the leaf count does not, and this is the relation that says
#   so.
-- composition/descent.sqlc reduced to one row per reached layer, against rank/composition_kernel.sqlc.
SELECT r.composition,
       r.composed_layer,
       coalesce(b.deepest, 0)                             AS deepest,
       1 + coalesce(b.fusions, 0)                         AS fusions_below,
       coalesce(b.leaves, 0)                              AS leaves,
       1::bigint                                          AS row_dim,
       coalesce(b.leaves, 0) - 1                          AS closure_kernel,
       r.kernel_dim + coalesce(b.kernel, 0)               AS sum_kernel,
       coalesce(b.leaves_positive, 0)                     AS leaves_positive,
       coalesce(b.leaves_unknown, 0)                      AS leaves_unknown
FROM      (
    :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT w.root_filing,
           w.root_layer,
           max(w.depth)::bigint                                  AS deepest,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NOT NULL)      AS fusions,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NULL)          AS leaves,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NULL
                                  AND NOT w.factor_absent
                                  AND w.factor_mode > 0)          AS leaves_positive,
           count(*) FILTER (WHERE k.composition IS NULL
                                  AND w.factor_absent)            AS leaves_unknown,
           coalesce(sum(k.kernel_dim), 0)::bigint                 AS kernel
    FROM      (
        SELECT d.root_filing, d.root_layer, d.filing, d.layer,
               max(d.depth)             AS depth,
               bool_or(d.factor_absent) AS factor_absent,
               min(d.factor_mode)       AS factor_mode
        FROM (
            :compose(composition/descent.sqlc)
        ) d
        GROUP BY d.root_filing, d.root_layer, d.filing, d.layer
    ) w
    LEFT JOIN (
        :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc)
    ) k ON k.composition = w.filing AND k.composed_layer = w.layer
    GROUP BY w.root_filing, w.root_layer
) b ON b.root_filing = r.composition AND b.root_layer = r.composed_layer
